# Wrist camera names for elroy (the Orin). Install as:
#   sudo cp config/99-cameras-elroy.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/99-cameras.rules
#   sudo udevadm control --reload-rules
#   sudo udevadm trigger --action=add --subsystem-match=video4linux
#   ls -l /dev/cam_*
#
# --action=add IS NOT OPTIONAL. Plain `udevadm trigger` fires CHANGE events,
# and these rules do not take effect on a change - reload, trigger, and get
# no symlink, with no error anywhere. Replugging the camera works too, for
# the same reason: that is a real add event.
#
# WHY POSITIONAL. Both Innomaker U20CAM-1080p-S1 report serial SN0001, so
# /dev/v4l/by-id collides and only one entry is ever created. Nothing in the
# cameras' identity distinguishes them; where they are plugged in is the
# only thing that does.
#
# WHY THE CAPABILITY FILTER. Each camera creates TWO video nodes sharing one
# ID_PATH - a capture node and a metadata node. Without
# ID_V4L_CAPABILITIES the symlink lands on whichever udev handles last, and
# roughly half the time you get a camera that opens fine and never delivers
# a frame.
#
# WHY ID_PATH AND NOT KERNELS. On the Pi a KERNELS rule stopped working
# after a camera changed ports and would not come back even once the value
# was corrected - udevadm test showed the right value in the device chain
# and created no symlink.
#
# That was probably the trigger action, not the KERNELS key: the same
# symptom turned up here with a rule that was correct in every respect, and
# `--action=add` fixed it. Not proven for the Pi - if those ID_PATH rules
# really did take with a plain trigger, something else was going on - but
# check the action before blaming the match next time.
#
# MEASURED 2026-10-09 on elroy, with both cameras on the external hub:
#
#   platform-3610000.usb   the Tegra xHCI controller (stable across boots)
#   usb-0:2.3.3            root port 2 -> internal hub port 3 -> hub port 3
#   usb-0:2.3.4            root port 2 -> internal hub port 3 -> hub port 4
#
# These encode YOUR HUB'S PORTS. Move a camera to a different hub port, or
# the hub to a different Type-A port, and the path changes and the rule
# silently stops matching. Check `ls -l /dev/cam_*` after any replug.

SUBSYSTEM=="video4linux", ENV{ID_PATH}=="platform-3610000.usb-usb-0:2.3.3:1.0", ENV{ID_V4L_CAPABILITIES}=="*:capture:*", GROUP="video", MODE="0660", SYMLINK+="cam_left"
SUBSYSTEM=="video4linux", ENV{ID_PATH}=="platform-3610000.usb-usb-0:2.3.4:1.0", ENV{ID_V4L_CAPABILITIES}=="*:capture:*", GROUP="video", MODE="0660", SYMLINK+="cam_right"

# The head RealSense needs no rule here: it is addressed by serial number
# (836112060112), not a device path, so it can move ports freely. It may
# still need librealsense's own permissions rules - see ORIN-SETUP.md.
